\begin{abstract}
We introduce the \emph{Reversible Scatter Flow} (\RSF{}) architecture,
a fifth-paradigm deep neural network design built entirely from
\emph{invertible affine coupling primitives}.
Unlike convolutional networks, Transformers, or recurrent architectures,
every layer in an \RSF{} model is a bijective transformation,
enabling \emph{exact} reconstruction of any hidden state from the
output alone.
This property reduces backpropagation memory from $\mathcal{O}(L)$
to $\mathcal{O}(1)$ in the number of layers $L$,
dramatically lowering GPU VRAM requirements without
any approximation or gradient checkpointing heuristic.

Beyond the memory advantage, \RSF{} is the first large-scale neural
architecture whose mathematical invariants—invertibility,
shape safety, and memory safety—are simultaneously verified by
\emph{four independent proof assistants}: \Lean{}, \Twelf{},
\Beluga{}, and \Mizar{}.
The runtime is implemented in \Zig{} for memory-safe systems
programming, and GPU kernels are written in \Futhark{} for
data-parallel acceleration on NVIDIA CUDA and OpenCL hardware.
Distributed training across multiple GPUs is supported via
NCCL collective communication, and a cloud deployment layer
targets Modal B300/B200 GPU clusters.

We present the mathematical foundations of the architecture,
its engineering realisation, formal correctness guarantees,
and discuss theoretical advantages over standard lossy architectures.
\end{abstract}

