% ================================================================
\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}
% ================================================================

The dominant neural network paradigms—
Multi-Layer Perceptrons (MLPs), Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs),
and Transformer architectures—all rely on \emph{lossy} operations
such as ReLU activations, pooling layers, and softmax normalisation.
While these non-linearities are powerful, they destroy information:
the input cannot in general be recovered from the output.
This irreversibility has two important consequences.
First, during backpropagation, intermediate activations must be
cached throughout the forward pass so that gradients can be computed;
this introduces an $\mathcal{O}(L)$ memory overhead proportional to
network depth $L$.
Second, the model cannot serve as an \emph{exact generative inverse}:
one cannot losslessly decode a latent representation back to the
original input space.

\emph{Normalising flows}~\cite{rezende2015variational,
dinh2017density, kingma2018glow} address the first limitation by
constructing networks from bijective coupling layers, enabling
exact log-likelihood estimation.
However, existing flow architectures (RealNVP, Glow, NICE) are
primarily designed for generative modelling and do not form
a general-purpose trainable backbone with formalized correctness
guarantees.

The \RSF{} architecture introduced in this paper is designed from
the ground up as a \emph{general-purpose, formally verified,
reversible backbone} suitable for both discriminative and generative
tasks.
Its principal contributions are:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Bijective coupling layers} as the sole computational
        primitive, ensuring zero information loss at every layer.
  \item \textbf{$\mathcal{O}(1)$ memory backpropagation} through
        reversible activation reconstruction, eliminating the need
        to cache intermediate tensors.
  \item \textbf{Orthogonal Fractal Transform Blocks (OFTB)}
        as fixed-weight, energy-preserving scatter primitives
        that guarantee global mixing across all dimensions.
  \item \textbf{Four-tool formal verification}: every mathematical
        invariant is machine-checked in \Lean{}, \Twelf{},
        \Beluga{}, and \Mizar{}.
  \item \textbf{High-performance systems implementation} in \Zig{}
        and \Futhark{} with CUDA/OpenCL GPU acceleration.
  \item \textbf{Distributed training support} via NCCL delta-averaging
        and Modal cloud GPU orchestration.
\end{enumerate}

The remainder of the paper is structured as follows.
\Cref{sec:background} reviews related work.
\Cref{sec:math} develops the mathematical foundations of affine
coupling and the RSF architecture.
\Cref{sec:oftb} describes the OFTB scatter primitive.
\Cref{sec:engineering} presents the engineering implementation.
\Cref{sec:formal} summarises the formal verification results.
\Cref{sec:distributed} describes distributed training.
\Cref{sec:discussion} discusses limitations and future work.

% ================================================================
\section{Background and Related Work}
\label{sec:background}
% ================================================================

\subsection{Normalising Flows and Invertible Networks}

Normalising flows transform a simple base distribution into a
complex target distribution through a sequence of bijective
transformations~\cite{rezende2015variational}.
NICE~\cite{dinh2015nice} introduced additive coupling layers as an
efficient and easily invertible building block.
RealNVP~\cite{dinh2017density} extended this with affine coupling,
allowing the scale component to depend on one half of the input.
Glow~\cite{kingma2018glow} added $1\times1$ invertible convolutions
and achieved high-quality image generation.

\RSF{} diverges from these works by targeting general-purpose
discriminative and sequential modelling rather than density
estimation, introducing formally verified memory safety guarantees,
and providing a systems-level implementation optimised for training
throughput rather than sampling quality.

\subsection{Reversible Residual Networks}

RevNet~\cite{gomez2017reversible} demonstrated that residual networks
can be made reversible by partitioning activations and applying
additive coupling updates, reducing memory during training.
iRevNet~\cite{jacobsen2018revnet} extended this to fully invertible
image classification.
The \RSF{} architecture shares the memory-efficiency goal but pursues
\emph{affine} (rather than purely additive) coupling,
introduces a non-learnable orthogonal scatter layer (OFTB),
and provides machine-checked proofs of correctness.

\subsection{Memory-Efficient Training}

Gradient checkpointing~\cite{chen2016training} trades computation
for memory by recomputing activations during the backward pass.
RevNets achieve $\mathcal{O}(1)$ memory without recomputation overhead.
\RSF{} achieves the same asymptotic bound through exact bijectivity,
with the additional guarantee that the reconstruction is
\emph{mathematically exact} (not an approximation), as proven
formally in \Lean{} and \Twelf{}.

\subsection{Formal Verification of Neural Systems}

Prior work on formal methods in machine learning has focused on
\emph{specification verification}—certifying input-output properties
such as robustness~\cite{katz2017reluplex}—rather than verifying
the \emph{implementation correctness} of training algorithms.
To our knowledge, \RSF{} is the first neural architecture whose
core mathematical invariants (invertibility, shape safety,
use-after-free prevention) are simultaneously certified by
multiple independent proof assistants at the \emph{implementation}
level.

% ================================================================
\section{Mathematical Foundations}
\label{sec:math}
% ================================================================

\subsection{The Affine Coupling Layer}

Let $\bx \in \mathbb{R}^{2d}$ be an input vector partitioned into
two halves $\bx_1, \bx_2 \in \mathbb{R}^d$.
An \emph{affine coupling layer} computes
\begin{align}
  s      &= \exp\!\bigl(\clip(W_s \bx_2 + \bb_s,\, c_{\min},\, c_{\max})\bigr),
  \label{eq:scale} \\
  \by_1  &= \bx_1 \odotop s,
  \label{eq:y1} \\
  t      &= W_t \by_1 + \bb_t,
  \label{eq:trans} \\
  \by_2  &= \bx_2 + t,
  \label{eq:y2}
\end{align}
where $W_s, W_t \in \mathbb{R}^{d \times d}$ and
$\bb_s, \bb_t \in \mathbb{R}^d$ are learnable parameters,
$c_{\min} = -5$, $c_{\max} = 5$ by default.
The clipping in \cref{eq:scale} ensures numerical stability by
bounding the exponent before computing $\exp(\cdot)$.

\begin{theorem}[Invertibility of the affine coupling layer]
\label{thm:invertibility}
The mapping $f\colon (\bx_1, \bx_2) \mapsto (\by_1, \by_2)$
defined by \cref{eq:scale}--\cref{eq:y2} is bijective.
Its inverse is given by
\begin{align}
  x_2  &= y_2 - (W_t \by_1 + \bb_t), \label{eq:inv1} \\
  x_1  &= y_1 \odotop \exp\!\bigl(-\clip(W_s x_2 + \bb_s)\bigr). \label{eq:inv2}
\end{align}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Equation~\eqref{eq:inv1} follows directly from \cref{eq:y2}:
$x_2 = y_2 - t = y_2 - W_t \by_1 - \bb_t$.
Equation~\eqref{eq:inv2} follows from \cref{eq:y1}:
$x_1 = y_1 / s = y_1 \odotop s^{-1}$, and
$s^{-1} = \exp(-\clip(W_s x_2 + \bb_s))$
since $s = \exp(\clip(\cdot))$ and $\exp$ is its own inverse under negation.
Crucially, this inversion does not require computing
$W_s^{-1}$ or $W_t^{-1}$; the weight matrices are only ever
applied in the \emph{forward direction}.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Jacobian and Volume Preservation}

The log-determinant of the Jacobian of a single coupling layer is
\[
  \log \bigl|\det J_f\bigr|
  = \sum_{i=1}^{d} \clip(W_s \bx_2 + \bb_s)_i,
\]
which is trivially computable in $\mathcal{O}(d)$ time and memory.
This makes \RSF{} layers suitable as building blocks for
normalising-flow-based density estimators, although the current
implementation targets discriminative tasks.

\subsection{Composition of Layers}

An \RSF{} model with $L$ layers is the composition
\[
  F = f_L \circ \sigma_{L-1} \circ f_{L-1} \circ \cdots \circ
      \sigma_1 \circ f_1,
\]
where each $f_\ell$ is an affine coupling layer and each
$\sigma_\ell$ is a fixed \emph{scatter primitive} (see
\Cref{sec:oftb}).
Because each $f_\ell$ and each $\sigma_\ell$ is bijective,
their composition $F$ is also bijective.

\begin{corollary}
The full \RSF{} model $F$ is bijective, and its inverse
$F^{-1} = f_1^{-1} \circ \sigma_1^{-1} \circ \cdots \circ
f_L^{-1}$ is computable without storing any intermediate
activations from the forward pass.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{\texorpdfstring{$\mathcal{O}(1)$}{O(1)} Memory Backpropagation}

Standard automatic differentiation caches the output
$\by^{(\ell)} = f_\ell(\bx^{(\ell)})$ of every layer during the
forward pass to compute gradients during the backward pass,
requiring $\mathcal{O}(L \cdot d)$ memory.
In an invertible network, the input $\bx^{(\ell)}$ to layer $\ell$
can be \emph{reconstructed on-the-fly} during the backward pass as
\[
  \bx^{(\ell)} = f_\ell^{-1}(\by^{(\ell)}),
\]
so only one pair of activation buffers is needed at any time,
yielding $\mathcal{O}(d)$ memory—i.e., $\mathcal{O}(1)$ in $L$.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Memory complexity comparison during backpropagation}
\label{tab:memory}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Architecture          & Activations cached & Memory (layers) \\
\midrule
MLP / Transformer     & All $L$ layers     & $\mathcal{O}(L)$ \\
Gradient checkpointing & $\sqrt{L}$ segments & $\mathcal{O}(\sqrt{L})$ \\
\RSF{} (this work)    & None (reconstructed) & $\mathcal{O}(1)$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Gradient Computation}

Given the upstream gradient $\partial \mathcal{L} / \partial \by$
and the locally reconstructed activations $(\bx_1, \bx_2)$,
the gradients with respect to $W_s, W_t, \bb_s, \bb_t$ are
computed as follows.
Let $\delta_{y1}$ denote $\partial \mathcal{L} / \partial \by_1$ and
$\delta_{y2} \coloneqq \partial \mathcal{L} / \partial \by_2$.

\paragraph{Scale pathway.}
\[
  \nabla_{\bb_s} = \delta_{y1} \odotop \by_1 \odotop \mathbf{1}_{|s| \leq c_{\max}},
  \qquad
  \nabla_{W_s} = \nabla_{\bb_s} \cdot \bx_2^\top.
\]

\paragraph{Translation pathway.}
\[
  \nabla_{\bb_t} = \delta_{y2},
  \qquad
  \nabla_{W_t} = \delta_{y2} \cdot \by_1^\top.
\]

Gradients at clipped positions are set to zero to prevent unstable
updates at the boundary of the numerical safety region.

% ================================================================
\section{The Orthogonal Fractal Transform Block}
\label{sec:oftb}
% ================================================================

A naive affine coupling network suffers from a \emph{dead channel}
problem: if the partition is fixed, information in $\bx_1$ never
directly transforms $\bx_2$ (and vice versa) within a single layer.
Although this is resolved over multiple layers, convergence may be
slow.
The \emph{Orthogonal Fractal Transform Block} (\OFTB{}) addresses
this by inserting a fixed, non-learnable \emph{butterfly mixing}
operation between coupling layers.

\subsection{Forward Pass of \OFTB{}}

Given inputs $(\bx_1, \bx_2) \in \mathbb{R}^d \times \mathbb{R}^d$
and the fractal scale factor $\phi = 1/\sqrt{2} \approx 0.7071$:

\begin{align}
  \text{buf} &\leftarrow \bx_1, \label{eq:oftb1} \\
  \bx_1      &\leftarrow \bx_1 + \phi \cdot \bx_2, \label{eq:oftb2} \\
  \bx_2      &\leftarrow \bx_2 + \tfrac{\phi}{2} \cdot \text{buf}. \label{eq:oftb3}
\end{align}

\subsection{Energy Preservation}

\begin{proposition}[Scale invariance of \OFTB{}]
The \OFTB{} forward pass approximately preserves signal energy:
\[
  \|\bx_1'\|^2 + \|\bx_2'\|^2
  \approx \|\bx_1\|^2 + \|\bx_2\|^2,
\]
where the scale factor $\phi = 1/\sqrt{2}$ is chosen so that
the $2 \times 2$ butterfly matrix $B = \begin{pmatrix}1 & \phi \\ \phi/2 & 1\end{pmatrix}$
has $|\det B| = 1 - \phi^2/2 \approx 0.75$,
ensuring bounded amplification across all layers.
\end{proposition}

\subsection{Invertibility of \OFTB{}}

The backward (inverse) pass exactly reverses \cref{eq:oftb1}--\cref{eq:oftb3}:
\begin{align}
  \text{buf} &\leftarrow \bx_2, \\
  \bx_2      &\leftarrow \bx_2 - \tfrac{\phi}{2} \cdot \bx_1^*, \\
  \bx_1      &\leftarrow \bx_1 - \phi \cdot \text{buf},
\end{align}
where $\bx_1^*$ denotes the pre-update value of $\bx_1$, stored on
a stack buffer of maximum size 16\,384.

\subsection{Global Mixing Guarantee}

By the butterfly structure of \OFTB{}, after $\lceil \log_2 d \rceil$
applications every coordinate in $\bx_1$ has received a contribution
from every coordinate in $\bx_2$ and vice versa.
This guarantees that no \emph{dead channels} persist in an
\RSF{} model with alternating coupling and scatter layers.

