  \node[box] (formal) at (0,-2)   {Proof Assistants};
  \node[box] (dist)   at (2.5,-4) {Distributed (NCCL + Modal)};

  \draw[arr] (zig) -- node[above]{\small FFI calls} (fut);
  \draw[arr] (fut) -- node[right]{\small compiles to} (cuda);
  \draw[arr] (zig) -- node[left]{\small verified by} (formal);
  \draw[arr] (zig) -- node[left,xshift=-0.3cm]{\small orchestrates} (dist);
  \draw[arr] (fut) -- node[right,xshift=0.3cm]{\small powers} (dist);
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{High-level component diagram of the \RSF{} system.}
\label{fig:architecture}
\end{figure}

The \RSF{} system is structured as five interlocking layers:
(1)~a \Zig{} runtime managing model lifecycle and CPU-side logic;
(2)~\Futhark{} GPU kernels providing data-parallel acceleration;
(3)~CUDA/OpenCL bindings for device memory and streams;
(4)~a distributed training layer using NCCL and Modal;
(5)~formal proofs across four proof assistants.

\subsection{\Zig{} Runtime: Handle/Core Registry}

The runtime adopts a \emph{handle/core registry} pattern to provide
both a stable public API and thread-safe internal state management.

\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{RSF} — top-level model handle (public API).
  \item \texttt{RSFCore} — internal model state;
        holds an array of \texttt{LayerCore} objects.
  \item \texttt{RSFLayer} — handle for a single coupling layer.
  \item \texttt{LayerCore} — raw tensor data: weights $W_s, W_t$,
        biases $\bb_s, \bb_t$, and gradient accumulators.
\end{itemize}

Reference counting is implemented with \texttt{acquireCore()}
(increment) and \texttt{releaseCore()} (decrement; free if zero and
marked for destruction).
A \texttt{std.Thread.RwLock} ensures that weight updates (writes)
do not race with forward/inverse passes (reads).

\subsubsection{Initialisation Protocol}

Before allocation, \texttt{validateClipRange} asserts that
clip bounds lie in $[-20.0, 20.0]$ and
\texttt{validateModelConfigValues} checks that dimensions and layer
counts are positive and within hardware limits.
Initialisation follows an all-or-nothing pattern using
\texttt{errdefer} to guarantee that a partially constructed model
is always fully torn down on any error.

\subsection{Model Persistence: RSF0 Binary Format}

Trained models are serialised to a custom binary format
(magic: \texttt{RSF0}, current version: 4).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{RSF0 file header layout}
\label{tab:header}
\begin{tabular}{llll}
\toprule
Offset & Type     & Field       & Notes \\
\midrule
0      & \texttt{[4]u8} & Magic   & \texttt{RSF0} \\
4      & \texttt{u32}   & Version & Currently 4 \\
8      & \texttt{u64}   & Dimension & Model width \\
16     & \texttt{u64}   & Layers  & Number of coupling layers \\
24     & \texttt{f32}   & Clip Min & Typically $-5.0$ \\
28     & \texttt{f32}   & Clip Max & Typically $5.0$ \\
32     & \texttt{u8}    & Grad Mean flag & \\
33     & \texttt{[7]u8} & Padding & Alignment \\
40     & \texttt{u32}   & CRC32   & Header integrity \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The payload contains, for each layer in order:
$s\_\text{weight}$, $t\_\text{weight}$, $s\_\text{bias}$, $t\_\text{bias}$
as raw \texttt{f32} arrays.
Saves are atomic: data is first written to a temporary file,
CRC32 is computed, \texttt{fsync} is called, then the file is
renamed atomically via \texttt{std.fs.Dir.rename}.

\subsection{\Futhark{} GPU Kernels}

\Futhark{}~\cite{henriksen2017futhark} is a purely functional,
data-parallel programming language that compiles to optimised
CUDA or OpenCL C code.
\RSF{} uses \Futhark{} for all numerically intensive operations.

\subsubsection{Core Entry Points}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{rsf\_forward}: Computes affine coupling forward pass
        (\cref{eq:scale}--\cref{eq:y2}).
  \item \texttt{rsf\_backward}: Computes gradients
        $\nabla W_s, \nabla W_t, \nabla \bb_s, \nabla \bb_t$
        without storing intermediate activations.
  \item \texttt{rsf\_scatter}: Applies the \OFTB{} mixing with
        scale factor $\phi = 1/\sqrt{2}$.
  \item \texttt{training\_step}: Fused kernel executing
        batch forward, MSE loss, gradient computation, and
        momentum-based optimiser update in a single kernel dispatch.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Auxiliary Kernels}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{xavier\_fill\_inplace}: Xavier/Glorot uniform weight
        initialisation.
  \item \texttt{matmul\_tiled} / \texttt{batched\_matmul}: Tiled
        matrix multiplication for cache efficiency.
  \item \texttt{spectral\_clip}: Fisher information matrix clipping.
  \item \texttt{dot\_product}: NaN-tolerant scalar product.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{CUDA Memory Management}

Device memory (\texttt{cudaMalloc} / \texttt{cudaFree}) is used for
model weights and gradient buffers.
Pinned (page-locked) host memory (\texttt{cudaHostAlloc}) enables
asynchronous DMA transfers so CPU and GPU computation overlap:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{cudaHostAllocDefault}: Standard pinned memory.
  \item \texttt{cudaHostAllocMapped}: Mapped into CUDA address space.
  \item \texttt{cudaHostAllocWriteCombined}: Write-combined for
        CPU-to-GPU upload paths.
\end{itemize}

CUDA streams (\texttt{cudaStream\_t}) serialise asynchronous operations;
\texttt{cudaStreamSynchronize} blocks until a specific stream completes,
while \texttt{cudaDeviceSynchronize} waits for all pending operations.

\subsection{Fractal LPU: Hierarchical Compute Scheduler}

The \emph{Fractal Logical Processing Unit} (Fractal LPU) manages
large-scale parallelism through a recursive quad-tree
(\texttt{FractalTile}) structure.

Key configuration parameters:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{hausdorff\_dim}: Theoretical density of the
        computational mapping.
  \item \texttt{box\_counting\_levels}: Maximum quad-tree recursion
        depth.
  \item \texttt{coherence\_threshold}: Minimum signal stability
        required to activate a tile.
  \item \texttt{min\_tile\_size} / \texttt{max\_tile\_size}:
        Memory footprint bounds per segment.
\end{itemize}

Load balancing iterates over all \texttt{ComputeUnit} objects,
computes average \texttt{pending\_ops}, and clips outliers by a
\texttt{load\_balance\_factor}.
Fixed-point arithmetic (16.16 format) is used for deterministic
results across CPU architectures and GPU vendors.

