\subsection{\Lean{} 4 Proofs}

\subsubsection{Invertibility}

The central theorem verified in \Lean{} is:

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{ThForwardInverseIdentity}]
For all inputs $\bx \in \mathbb{R}^{2d}$:
\[
  \text{Inverse}(\text{Forward}(\bx)) = \bx.
\]
\end{theorem}

The proof proceeds by symbolic rewriting: expanding the definitions
of \texttt{ForwardInPlace} and \texttt{InverseInPlace},
then applying the arithmetic identities
$(a \cdot e^c) \cdot e^{-c} = a$ and
$(b + t) - t = b$.
This directly corresponds to \cref{thm:invertibility} above.

\subsubsection{\OFTB{} Correctness}

Three additional theorems are proven for the \OFTB{} layer:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{forwardPass\_eq\_iterative\_strict}: The $N$-stage
        butterfly implementation is definitionally equal to the
        recursive fractal specification.
  \item \texttt{det\_forward\_eq\_det\_backward}: The Jacobian
        determinant is preserved across the forward pass—a necessary
        condition for flow-based models.
  \item \texttt{safety\_preserved\_forward}: The \texttt{OFTBState}
        invariants (valid pointer ranges, non-null buffers)
        are preserved through the transformation.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Embedding Safety}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{weightIdx\_no\_usize\_overflow}]
If \texttt{vocab\_size} $\times$ \texttt{dim} $<$ \texttt{USIZE\_BOUND},
then for any valid token index $t < \text{vocab\_size}$ and
dimension $d < \text{dim}$, the flat index
$t \cdot \text{dim} + d$ does not overflow a \texttt{usize}.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{\Twelf{} Proofs}

The \Twelf{} formalisation encodes judgements in a
\emph{logical framework} style, enabling structural proof search.

\subsubsection{Arithmetic Invertibility}

Core arithmetic laws are encoded as judgements:
\begin{align*}
  \texttt{add-sub-cancel}&\colon (A + B) - B = A, \\
  \texttt{mul-div-cancel}&\colon (A \times B) / B = A \quad (B \neq 0).
\end{align*}
These form the primitive building blocks of the coupling layer
invertibility proof.

\subsubsection{Memory Model: No-Alias Registry}

The \texttt{split-judgment} encodes the following invariant:
\emph{when a tensor is partitioned into handles $h_1$ and $h_2$,
the underlying memory ranges are disjoint}.
This prevents race conditions in \Futhark{} kernels where both
halves of a split tensor might be read or written concurrently.

\subsubsection{GPU State Versioning}

The \texttt{gpu-sync-op} judgement formalises the transition
\texttt{CPU\_DIRTY} $\to$ \texttt{GPU\_CLEAN}, ensuring that
no kernel is launched on stale device data.
The \texttt{weight-update-op} judgement mirrors the
\texttt{active\_ops} counter and \texttt{RwLock} mechanism,
proving that concurrent weight updates are not possible during
active forward/backward passes.

\subsubsection{Top-Level Invertibility}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{top-level-invertibility-theorem}]
For any sequence of layers $f_1, \ldots, f_L$,
there exists a corresponding sequence of inverse operations
$f_1^{-1}, \ldots, f_L^{-1}$ such that their composition is
the identity.
\end{theorem}

The proof is by structural induction: the base case is the empty
sequence (identity map); the inductive step appends one coupling
layer and appeals to \texttt{add-sub-cancel} and
\texttt{mul-div-cancel} for the affine and exponential components.

\subsection{\Beluga{} Proofs}

\Beluga{} operates in \emph{contextual type theory}, enabling
fine-grained reasoning about variable binding and dependent types.

\subsubsection{Registry Safety (No Use-After-Free)}

Valid state transitions are encoded as:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{tr-acquire}: Increments the live core reference count.
  \item \texttt{tr-release-final}: Reference count $= 1$ transitions
        to \texttt{reg-freed} (if marked for destruction).
  \item \texttt{tr-destroy-zero}: Immediately frees if
        reference count $= 0$ and destruction is pending.
\end{itemize}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{RegistryNoUseAfterFreeW}]
If a core is in state \texttt{reg-freed}, no \texttt{tr-acquire}
transition is reachable.
\end{theorem}

\subsubsection{Shape Invariants}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{completeShapePreservationTheorem}]
For any valid model configuration with dimensions
\texttt{Batch} $\times$ (\texttt{Dim} $+$ \texttt{Dim}) $= \texttt{Full}$,
the tensor shapes are exactly reconstructed by
\texttt{InverseInPlace} after \texttt{ForwardInPlace}.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{completeIndexSafetyTheorem}]
For any valid indices $B < \texttt{Rows}$ and $D < \texttt{Cols}$,
the flat index $\texttt{Idx} = B \times \texttt{Cols} + D$
satisfies $\texttt{Idx} < \texttt{total}$.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{\Mizar{} Proofs}

\Mizar{} provides a set-theoretic foundation closest to
standard mathematical practice.
The \RSF{} \Mizar{} proofs cover:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Tensor well-formedness}:
        $\texttt{len}(T.\texttt{data}) = T.\texttt{rows} \times T.\texttt{cols}$.
  \item \textbf{Layer well-formedness} (\texttt{LC is well-formed}):
        dimension consistency and clip bounds $\in [-20.0, 20.0]$.
  \item \textbf{Xavier/Glorot initialisation correctness}:
        \texttt{xavier\_bound} $= \sqrt{6 / (\texttt{fan\_in} + \texttt{fan\_out})}$.
  \item \textbf{Serialisation specification} (\texttt{SerializeRSF}):
        the binary layout at version 4 exactly matches the
        \Zig{} persistence layer implementation.
  \item \textbf{Gradient pipeline safety}: gradients can be
        updated only after \texttt{EnsureGradients} initialisation.
\end{itemize}

