\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}
% ================================================================

\subsection{Advantages Over Lossy Architectures}

\paragraph{No information loss.}
Because every layer is bijective, information is never discarded.
This is particularly advantageous for tasks requiring
\emph{exact reconstruction}, such as lossless data compression,
reversible data augmentation, and certain scientific computing
applications where the input must be recoverable from any intermediate
representation.

\paragraph{Memory efficiency.}
The $\mathcal{O}(1)$ memory backpropagation allows training models
of arbitrary depth without exhausting GPU VRAM.
For very deep models (hundreds of layers), this is a qualitative
advantage over gradient checkpointing, which introduces a constant
factor recomputation overhead.

\paragraph{Formal correctness.}
The use of four independent proof assistants provides a high degree
of confidence in the implementation.
In particular, \Beluga{}'s proof of no-use-after-free at the
registry level, combined with \Twelf{}'s no-alias memory model,
addresses a class of bugs that is extremely difficult to test
for empirically but can have catastrophic consequences in production.

\subsection{Limitations and Future Work}

\paragraph{Expressivity.}
Bijective architectures are, by construction, volume-preserving
up to a learned scale factor.
This may limit their expressivity relative to arbitrary feedforward
networks for tasks that benefit from aggressive dimensionality
reduction in early layers.
Future work should explore learnable permutation layers and
structured sparse coupling patterns to increase effective capacity
without sacrificing invertibility.

\paragraph{Empirical benchmarking.}
The current paper presents the mathematical and engineering
foundations of \RSF{}.
A comprehensive empirical comparison against Transformer and
MLP-Mixer baselines on standard benchmarks (ImageNet, GLUE,
language modelling perplexity) is left as immediate future work.

\paragraph{Quantum-classical integration.}
The hybrid quantum training mode is highly experimental.
Future work should characterise the training dynamics of the
quantum-classical interface and evaluate whether VQE layers provide
a genuine gradient signal advantage on near-term hardware.

\paragraph{Extending formal coverage.}
The current proofs cover invertibility, shape safety, memory safety,
and serialisation.
Future targets include a fully machine-checked proof of the
$\mathcal{O}(1)$ memory theorem (currently proven by hand in
\Cref{sec:math}), verified NCCL delta-averaging convergence,
and \Coq{} proofs for cross-tool consistency.

% ================================================================
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
% ================================================================

We have presented the \emph{Reversible Scatter Flow} (\RSF{})
architecture, a novel neural network paradigm built entirely from
bijective affine coupling primitives.
\RSF{} achieves $\mathcal{O}(1)$ memory backpropagation by
leveraging exact invertibility to reconstruct activations on demand
during the backward pass.
It incorporates the \OFTB{} orthogonal scatter primitive to ensure
global information mixing without learnable parameters.
The entire system is implemented in memory-safe \Zig{} with
data-parallel \Futhark{} GPU kernels, supports multi-GPU distributed
training via NCCL delta-averaging, and provides machine-checked
formal proofs of invertibility, shape safety, memory safety, and
serialisation correctness across four independent proof assistants.

The combination of theoretical elegance, engineering rigour, and
formal verification makes \RSF{} a promising foundation for
next-generation neural architectures where correctness,
interpretability, and hardware efficiency are simultaneously
required.

